otal": 7.23, "baseline_margin": 2.23, "analyst_adjustment": 0.57, "key_factors": ["Strong Democratic midterm environment pressing a first-term incumbent in an R+4 district", "Kalshi and Polymarket price Cognetti at 72-73%, ahead of the Tossup-heavy expert consensus", "Cognetti's fundraising edge and Giffords PAC advertising against Bresnahan", "Only public polls are Democratic-sponsored and dated, showing Cognetti by 1-2 points"], "rationale": "Bresnahan holds a Republican-leaning seat, but the midterm environment, Cognetti's fundraising advantage, and outside spending against him tilt a Tossup-rated race narrowly toward Democrats. Prediction markets at 72-73% for Cognetti, along with several ratings calling the seat a Democratic flip, justify a slightly stronger Democratic edge than the expert consensus alone implies.", "watch": "A late shift in the national mood or a heavy Republican outside-spending push could move this narrowly contested district back to the incumbent.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Rob Bresnahan (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Northeastern Pennsylvania's 8th District pairs a Republican-leaning electorate with a first-term Republican incumbent, Rob Bresnahan, running in a midterm environment that currently favors Democrats. Challenger Paige Cognetti has out-raised him roughly $7.1 million to $6.2 million, with near-equal cash on hand, and outside groups including Giffords PAC are funding advertising against the incumbent.\n\nExpert ratings cluster around Tossup, with several outlets calling the seat a Democratic flip. The only public polls, all sponsored by Democratic-leaning firms, show Cognetti narrowly ahead by one to two points, though they are dated. Prediction markets on Kalshi and Polymarket price the Democratic nominee near 72-73 percent, stronger than the ratings alone. The forecast lands on a narrow Democratic edge in what remains a